BACKUP’s interventions supporting GF financing focus on three core areas of intervention, derived from the RSSH Modular Framework of the Global Fund (thereby feeding directly into the GF RSSH strategy):
(i)Strengthening community engagement (e.g. new forms of participation in CCMs, Community-led Monitoring, effective linkages etc), with the aim to facilitate a shift from disease-specific community engagement towards systems thinking;
(ii)Improving governance and coordination (e.g. new/improved oversight functions, methods for resource mapping and monitoring of GHI funding etc); 
(iii)Providing people-centred health services (e.g. intersectoral planning; procedures for assessing and monitoring the integration of HTM services etc).

To bring likeminded countries together to resist rollback on sexual and reproductive health and rights at the multilateral level. This will complement existing work led by civil society partners. It will: 
1.Raise awareness of how pushback at the multilateral level impacts upon women’s rights at the local level;
2.Draw attention on the need for additional resources and support for women’s rights organisations and movements.

Two complementary events to celebrate International Women’s Day in March 2023 – one morning workshop (research dissemination) and one evening event (focus on women in research, science, tech and innovation) held in partnership with the British Chamber of Commerce. 

1. Disseminating results of the UK-Funded GESI Analysis for Kenya and getting feedback from key stakeholders to strengthen the analysis; 

2. Highlighting the UK’s commitment to Gender Equality and its work with women and girls across Kenya (opportunity to showcase some of our programmes and bring together UK-funded partners from different sectors); 

3. Stimulating dialogue, partnerships and collective action on Gender Equality in Kenya by bringing together key stakeholders around the focus areas of research, innovation and technology.
